  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will linger into Wednesday, keeping dry conditions\nand plenty of sunshine across the area. As this high pressure\ndrifts east midweek, a weak low pressure system centered near\nthe Gulf Coast will move north into the Ohio Valley. This system\nwill bring scattered showers and thunderstorms to the eastern\nportion of the CWA Wednesday afternoon into the evening. Given\nthe positioning of the low and the higher PWAT axis further\neast, there remains a decent chance that the bulk of the area\nremains dry. Given the overall weak forcing with this system,\nnot anticipating any severe weather at this point. \n\nLate Wednesday into Thursday, a broad upper level trough and\nassociated frontal boundary will approach the area from the\nwest. This frontal boundary will be slow moving and potentially\nstall near the area until pushing east on Friday. As a result,\nmultiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms are possible\nThursday and Friday. The confidence in any severe threat is low\nat this point given the discrepancies in timing amongst models,\nbut increasing dew points and daytime instability keep that\npotential around for the end of the week. In addition, will need\nto keep an eye on the potential for heavy rainfall as mean flow\nis expected to be near parallel to the frontal boundary which\ncould result in periods of training showers. Given the expected\nPWAT values and deep warm cloud layer, can also expect any\nstorms that develop to be associated with efficient rainfall\nrates. WPC has highlighted the entire area in a marginal ERO for\nThursday into Friday. The chance of showers will linger into the\nweekend as additional disturbances push across the area,\nalthough full spatial extent and timing remains uncertain. \n\nThrough the weekend, temperatures will remain near normal for\nthis time of year with highs climbing into the mid to upper 80s\nand overnight lows cooling into the 60s.",
